    scienceNote: "Frost does not 'descend' from the sky; it forms when water vapor near the ground deposits directly into ice crystals on objects whose surface temperature is below 0 °C. That is why frost always appears first on grass blades and tiles close to the ground — the name 'Frost's Descent' follows ancient usage, though in fact it is 'frost appears'. Radiative cooling is strongest on clear, calm nights, when frost forms most easily; hence the farming proverb 'frost strikes the hollows', for cold air pools in low-lying ground and frost damage there is often worse.",
    tip: "Frost grows up from the ground; it does not fall from the sky."
