    altNames: [],
    meaning: "The 'Collected Explanations of the Seventy-two Pentads' says: 'In the middle of the tenth month, rain falls and is pressed by the cold air, so it congeals into snow; minor is a word for what is not yet at full strength.' Minor Snow means the weather has turned cold and snow begins, but the amount is still small and the ground has not yet stored up deep cold. Astronomically, the sun's geocentric apparent ecliptic longitude reaches 240 degrees at this time, usually on November 22 or 23 in the Gregorian calendar. It is the second solar term of winter and, like Major Snow, is named for snow, describing a trend in the degree of cold and the form of precipitation rather than the actual weather on any one day. After Minor Snow, cold-air incursions southward grow ever stronger and the snow line gradually presses toward the south.",
    climate: "Around Minor Snow, northern temperatures continue to fall; the Yellow River basin receives its first snow, snow days increase in the Northeast and Northwest, and rivers and lakes freeze over one after another. The middle and lower Yangtze enter the damp-cold mode: gloomy rain with cutting wind makes it feel colder than the thermometer reads. South China is dry and rainless, formally entering its dry season with a widening day-night temperature gap. In the Northwest there is the hope expressed in 'If Minor Snow fills the sky, next year will surely be a bumper one' — the snow cover insulates winter wheat and preserves soil moisture. Most of the country does not actually snow on the day of Minor Snow itself, a point often misunderstood by those who read the name literally.",
