    meaning: "The 'Collected Explanations of the Seventy-two Pentads' says: 'Winter Solstice, mid-eleventh month. The qi of ending and storage reaches its extreme here.' At the moment of the Winter Solstice the sun's geocentric apparent ecliptic longitude reaches 270 degrees and its rays fall directly on the Tropic of Capricorn; this is the day with the shortest daylight and the longest night of the year in the Northern Hemisphere, usually on December 21 or 22 in the Gregorian calendar. Afterward the point of direct sunlight returns north and Northern Hemisphere days grow longer by the day; the ancients took this as an auspicious sign that yang energy was beginning to be born, calling it 'at the Winter Solstice, the first yang arises'. Since ancient times the Winter Solstice has borne the alternate names 'Changzhi' and 'Yasui' ('second to the New Year'); as early as the Han dynasty there was the custom of 'congratulating winter', with officials given holidays and merchants closing shop — hence the folk saying 'the Winter Solstice is as big as the New Year', and it was celebrated much like one.",
